course: everyday
lesson: 515
title: Mum on her own, Dad on his own
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In Leicester, Kezia's mum drives a bus and does everything else too: tea, homework, washing, school runs, the lot. Kezia's dad left when she was two. One night Kezia comes down for water and finds Mum asleep at the kitchen table on a pile of bills. Kezia puts a blanket over her and feels a lump in her throat. A single parent does the job of two people and gets very tired, and that is never because the child is «too much». Kezia can help with small jobs, tell Mum she loves her, and, if she's worried Mum is really struggling, tell another grown-up she trusts.
explain: Dads on their own learn fast. In Baltimore, Tremaine's dad does his cornrows (tight braids along the head) before school, badly. «Dad, that's tragic.» Dad laughs: «I'm learnin' from YouTube, give me a week.» In Dublin, Darragh's Da has been on his own since Mam died, and makes sausages and beans every single night. «Da, can we have something different?» «Teach me, so» (in Irish English «so» at the end means then). Now they cook together on Saturdays. In Belfast, Kian's dad tries to do Kian's sister's hair for a party: a disaster, until Kian finds a video and they do it together, all three laughing.
explain: One parent is a whole family. In Leeds, Lexi's teacher says, «Ask your mum or dad to sign this,» and Lexi thinks, «Just Mum, then.» Lots of teachers now say «your grown-up at home», because many kids live with one parent. In Toronto, Amani and her mom moved from Nairobi; «It's just you and me, kiddo,» says Mom, and every Friday is movie night with popcorn on the sofa. Small families can be very close. In Ohio, Zach has never met his dad. When kids ask, he says, «Never met him,» and shrugs. He's fine most days and curious some days, and he can ask Mom when he's ready.
explain: Single parents often need a team. In London, Elodie's mum is a nurse who works nights, so on those nights Elodie stays with Aunty Roisin, who makes the best stew in Peckham. In Swansea, Saffie's mam works two jobs, so Saffie gets home at quarter to four to an empty house till six; Mrs Hughes from next door pops round to check on her: «Alright, bach? Got everything, have you?» Saffie is lonely sometimes, but safe, and she knows exactly who to call.
explain: In Calgary, Brayden lives with his dad; his mom lives in BC (British Columbia, the province next door), and he sees her in the summer. On Mother's Day the class makes cards. Brayden makes his for Grandma, who picks him up every day. That's fine: you can make a card for whoever looks after you.
fact: Many children live with one parent, and one parent is a whole family.
fact: A single parent does the work of two people and often gets very tired; it is not because the child is too much.
fact: Children can help a single parent with small jobs and by saying they love them.
fact: If a child is worried that a parent is really struggling, telling another trusted grown-up is right.
fact: Single parents often learn new skills, like cooking or doing hair, and effort counts more than skill at first.
fact: Learning together, from each other or from videos, can make hard jobs fun.
fact: Many teachers say «your grown-up at home» because families come in many shapes.
fact: Single parents often rely on family, friends and neighbours to help with childcare.
fact: A child home alone for a while should know a trusted neighbour or grown-up to call.
fact: Some children never know one of their parents; feeling fine or curious are both normal.
fact: On Mother's Day or Father's Day, it's fine to make a card for whoever looks after you.
fact: Small families, like one parent and one child, can be very close.
fact: «Cornrows» are tight braids along the head; «so» at the end of an Irish sentence means then; «bach» is Welsh for little one; BC is British Columbia, a Canadian province; «tragic» is slang for really bad; «pure muck» is Irish for really rubbish.
exercise: What did Kezia find when she came down for water? | Mum asleep on a pile of bills | Mum watching telly and laughing | Mum cooking a big birthday cake | Mum talking to Dad on the phone
exercise: Why do single parents often get very tired? | They do the work of two people | Their children are too much for them | They stay up late watching films | They don't like their jobs very much
exercise: What can Kezia do to help? | Small jobs and telling Mum she loves her | Nothing, it's not a child's business at all | Pay all the bills from her pocket money | Get a job driving the bus at weekends
exercise: How is Tremaine's dad learning to do cornrows? | From YouTube | From a hairdresser's course | From Tremaine's mom | He isn't, he gave up
exercise: What did Darragh's Da mean by «Teach me, so»? | Teach me, then | Teach me, slowly | Don't teach me | Teach me, sorry
exercise: Why has Darragh's Da been on his own? | Mam died | Mam lives in Cork with her sister | Mam is working abroad for a year | Mam and Da are on a break
exercise: Lexi lives with her mum only. What do many teachers now say instead of «mum or dad»? | «Your grown-up at home» | «Your mum, obviously» | «Your dad, please» | «Your teacher at home»
exercise: Why does Elodie stay with Aunty Roisin some nights? | Mum is a nurse and works nights | Mum goes out to parties every night | Aunty Roisin's house is closer to school | Elodie doesn't like her own bedroom
exercise: Who checks on Saffie when she's home alone after school? | Mrs Hughes from next door | A policeman | Her teacher | Nobody at all
exercise: Brayden's mom lives in BC. What is BC? | British Columbia, a province of Canada | British Columbia, a town near London | A bus company in Calgary | A big city in the USA
exercise: Who did Brayden make his Mother's Day card for? | Grandma | His teacher | His mom in BC | Nobody
exercise: What do Amani and her mom do every Friday? | Movie night | Go to the gym together | Visit Nairobi | Clean the whole house
exercise: Zach has never met his dad. What does he say when kids ask? | «Never met him» | «He's a famous footballer in Spain» | «I don't want to talk to you ever» | «He's away on a secret mission»
exercise: How did Kian help his dad with his sister's hair? | Found a video and they did it together | Cut it all off so it would be easier | Told his sister to do it herself | Laughed and walked off
exercise: What does «tragic» mean when Tremaine says «Dad, that's tragic»? | Really bad | Really sad, like a death | Really good | Really long
test: Saffie gets home at 3:45 and Mam gets home at 6:00. Mrs Hughes pops round every 45 minutes, starting at 4:00. How many times does she pop round before Mam is home? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5
test: Tremaine's cornrows took Dad 50 minutes on Monday. Each school day he's 10 minutes quicker. On which day does it take 20 minutes? | Thursday | Wednesday | Friday | Tuesday
test: A kid at school: «Kezia's mum must be well lazy, she's always knackered». («knackered» means very tired.) What's true? | She does the work of two people | Tired people are always lazy | Kezia must be too much for her | Bus drivers don't work hard
test: Kezia finds Mum crying over bills for the third week running. What's the most helpful thing she could do? | Tell Mum she loves her, and tell a trusted grown-up she's worried | Keep it a secret from everyone so Mum isn't embarrassed | Hide the bills so Mum can't find them in the morning | Ask Mum for more pocket money to cheer her up
test: Darragh: «Da's cooking is pure muck, sausages AGAIN». What would most likely happen if they kept cooking together? | Da would learn new dinners and they'd have fun | Da would stop cooking for good | They'd only ever eat beans | Darragh would be told off for helping
test: «Lexi hasn't got a dad, so she's not a proper family». Right? | No, one parent is a whole family | Yes, you need two parents for a family | Yes, unless she has a stepdad | No, but she should pretend she has one
test: Zach: «I'm lowkey curious about my dad but I don't wanna upset Mom». What's a good plan? | Ask Mom when he's ready, gently | Never think about it ever again | Make up a dad and tell everyone | Search online for strangers called Dad
test: In a cartoon, the single dad is useless and burns everything forever. What's truer? | Single dads learn new skills, with effort | Dads can never learn to cook or braid | Only mums can look after children | Single dads always give up
test: Brayden: «Miss, can I make my Mother's Day card for my grandma? She does everything for me». What's right? | Yes, it's fine to make it for whoever looks after you | No, Mother's Day cards are for mothers only | Yes, but only if his mom says it's okay | No, he should skip it and sit at the back
test: Elodie's mum works 3 night shifts a week. Elodie stays at Aunty Roisin's on those nights. In 4 weeks, how many nights is that? | 12 | 7 | 3 | 28
test: «It's just you and me, kiddo». Amani's friend says that's sad. What's truer? | Small families can be very close | Two people can't be a family | Amani must be lonely every day | It's only fine if they get a pet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fatigue commonly experienced by a parent raising a child alone? | It arises from discharging the duties of two persons | It arises from the child being excessively burdensome | It is invariably a sign of indolence | It is a consequence of excessive leisure
exam: Identify the most appropriate conduct of a child who believes that a lone parent is struggling severely. | To assist with small tasks and inform a trusted adult | To conceal the matter from every other adult | To assume sole responsibility for the household bills | To demand additional pocket money from the parent
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate provision for a child who returns from school to an unoccupied dwelling for a period. | Knowledge of a trusted neighbour or adult to call | The prohibition of all contact with any adult | The requirement to remain outdoors until dusk | No provision of any kind being considered necessary
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a family consisting of one parent and one child. | It constitutes a complete family | It does not constitute a family | It constitutes a family only temporarily | It constitutes a family only with a pet
